<Project>
  <!--
    Portable native Skia + HarfBuzz binaries for all desktop RIDs. Referencing all three
    (win/linux/osx) makes a single build run on any desktop OS and lets you cross-publish;
    `dotnet publish -r <rid>` copies only the matching native. WebAssembly natives are
    provided separately by SkiaSharp.Views.Blazor in the web host, so they are NOT here.

    Imported by CupriFace (engine) and CupriFace.Shell. Keep versions in sync with the
    managed SkiaSharp / SkiaSharp.HarfBuzz packages.

    The -android condition below is DELIBERATELY LIMITED, and it is worth being honest about
    what it does not do. It evaluates against the IMPORTING project's TargetFramework, so it
    only helps a project that imports this file while targeting net10.0-android (none today).
    It does NOT stop the transitive restore of these desktop packages into an Android app that
    merely REFERENCES the engine — the engine itself builds as net10.0, so its dependency list
    always carries them (the Android probe observed exactly that). That leak is restore-only
    noise: RID-mismatched runtimes/<desktop-rid>/native assets cannot enter an APK, and the
    Android CI gate asserts as much by listing the APK's contents. The real fix would be
    multi-targeting the engine, which would tax every build everywhere with the android
    workload for a wart with no shipped-bytes consequence.
  -->
  <ItemGroup Condition="!$(TargetFramework.Contains('-android'))">
    <PackageReference Include="SkiaSharp.NativeAssets.Win32" Version="3.116.1" />
    <PackageReference Include="SkiaSharp.NativeAssets.Linux" Version="3.116.1" />
    <PackageReference Include="SkiaSharp.NativeAssets.macOS" Version="3.116.1" />
    <PackageReference Include="HarfBuzzSharp.NativeAssets.Win32" Version="8.3.0.1" />
    <PackageReference Include="HarfBuzzSharp.NativeAssets.Linux" Version="8.3.0.1" />
    <PackageReference Include="HarfBuzzSharp.NativeAssets.macOS" Version="8.3.0.1" />
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>
